3. Re-approving a delayed withdrawal gives the custodian one hour
instead of seven days 
Recommendation
One line fix. Clear the deadline when a revoked request is reopened, alongside the fields already cleared
there:
 function _reopenWithdrawalRequest(VaultTypes.WithdrawalRequest storage request) internal {
 request.status = VaultTypes.WithdrawalRequestStatus.Pending;
 request.approvedBy = address(0);
 request.completionTimestamp = 0;
+ request.approvalTimestamp = 0;
 pendingRequests++;
 }
Each new approval then gets its own full seven days and the permanent expiry problem disappears too.
If keeping the original deadline is genuinely intended, make it visible: return the remaining time from the
approval function, put it in the event, and reject any re-approval that would leave less than a minimum
settlement window. A custodian should never accept a request without knowing how long they have.
Preserving the first approval timestamp is deliberate. The purpose is to prevent an operator from
repeatedly revoking and reapproving a withdrawal to extend the customer’s escrow lock
indefinitely.
